import BlogPostLayout from "../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
const example = "<?php\ndeclare(strict_types=1);\n\nfunction lookupBarcode(string $barcode): ?array\n{\n    $barcode = trim($barcode);\n    if (!preg_match('/\\\\A(?:[0-9]{8}|[0-9]{12,14})\\\\z/', $barcode)) {\n        throw new InvalidArgumentException(\"Expected 8, 12, 13 or 14 digits\");\n    }\n    $apiKey = getenv(\"BARCODENEST_API_KEY\");\n    if ($apiKey === false || $apiKey === \"\") {\n        throw new RuntimeException(\"BARCODENEST_API_KEY is missing\");\n    }\n    $handle = curl_init(\n        \"https://api.barcodenest.com/v1/products/\" . rawurlencode($barcode)\n    );\n    if ($handle === false) {\n        throw new RuntimeException(\"Could not initialize cURL\");\n    }\n    try {\n        curl_setopt_array($handle, [\n            CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER => true,\n            CURLOPT_CONNECTTIMEOUT => 5,\n            CURLOPT_TIMEOUT => 30,\n            CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER => [\n                \"X-API-Key: \" . $apiKey,\n                \"Accept: application/json\",\n            ],\n        ]);\n        $body = curl_exec($handle);\n        if ($body === false) {\n            throw new RuntimeException(\"Barcode service connection failed\");\n        }\n        $status = curl_getinfo($handle, CURLINFO_HTTP_CODE);\n    } finally {\n        curl_close($handle);\n    }\n    if ($status === 404) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    if ($status !== 200) {\n        throw new RuntimeException(\"Barcode service returned HTTP \" . $status);\n    }\n    return json_decode($body, true, 512, JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR);\n}\n\n$result = lookupBarcode(\"3017620422003\");\nif ($result === null) {\n    echo \"No product record found.\" . PHP_EOL;\n} else {\n    $product = $result[\"product\"] ?? [];\n    echo ($product[\"name\"] ?? \"Name unavailable\") . PHP_EOL;\n    echo ($product[\"brand\"] ?? \"Brand unavailable\") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n";
---
<BlogPostLayout title="Barcode Lookup API in PHP with cURL: UPC and EAN Tutorial | BarcodeNest" heading="How to look up barcode product data in PHP with cURL" description="Add UPC and EAN product lookup to PHP with a server-side cURL example. Authenticate with an API key, handle missing products, and display optional fields." author="BarcodeNest Editorial Team" published="2026-11-19" readingTime="7 min read"><div class="article-shell container"><aside class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#setup">Set up the integration</a><a href="#request">Run the example</a><a href="#errors">Handle errors</a><a href="#next">Try your own data</a></aside><div class="article-body"><p>To look up a product by UPC or EAN in PHP, send the barcode string to a product data API from your server and read the JSON response. BarcodeNest's single lookup endpoint accepts EAN-8, UPC-A, EAN-13 and GTIN-14 identifiers. A matching record can supply a product name, brand, quantity and image URL, depending on the available data.</p>
<p>This tutorial builds a small PHP function you can use in a catalog administration page or an inventory form. It uses PHP's cURL extension and keeps the API key in the server environment.</p>
<h2 id="setup">Prepare PHP and your API key</h2>
<p>Use PHP 8 or later with the cURL extension enabled. Run <code>php --ri curl</code> to check the extension in your command-line environment. Your web server's PHP configuration may differ, so check that environment when connecting the example to a website.</p>
<p><a href="/register/">Create a BarcodeNest account</a> and generate an API key. Set <code>BARCODENEST_API_KEY</code> in the environment used by PHP. For a local PowerShell session, use <code>$env:BARCODENEST_API_KEY = "YOUR_API_KEY"</code>. For a macOS or Linux shell, use <code>export BARCODENEST_API_KEY="YOUR_API_KEY"</code>. Check the <a href="/docs/authentication/">authentication guide</a> when configuring your host.</p>
<h2 id="request">Make a barcode lookup request with cURL</h2>
<p>Save the following as <code>lookup.php</code> and run <code>php lookup.php</code>. The function validates the input's shape, sends the <code>X-API-Key</code> header and sets connection and total request timeouts. The API validates the check digit.</p>
<CodeBlock label="PHP barcode lookup" language="php" code={example} />
<p>The example barcode demonstrates the request format. The current record and its fields depend on available source data. A successful response contains a <code>product</code> object. See the <a href="/docs/barcode-lookup/">endpoint documentation</a> for the response fields.</p>
<h2 id="strings">Keep UPC and EAN values as strings</h2>
<p>Barcode values can start with zero. Pass them as quoted strings, store them in a text column and preserve their digits when importing spreadsheet data. An integer conversion can remove zeros before your application sends the request. The function accepts 8, 12, 13 or 14 ASCII digits and trims surrounding whitespace.</p>
<p>A barcode scanner that acts like a keyboard can fill a text input in your form. Your application can then submit that value to your own backend route, where the function runs. Converting an image into barcode digits requires an image decoder before this lookup step.</p>
<h2 id="errors">Handle missing products and request failures</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Response</th><th>What the function does</th><th>What to show the user</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200</td><td>Returns the decoded JSON.</td><td>A product form with available fields filled in.</td></tr><tr><td>404</td><td>Returns null.</td><td>A manual-entry option for the item.</td></tr><tr><td>400</td><td>Throws an exception.</td><td>A request to check the barcode digits.</td></tr><tr><td>401</td><td>Throws an exception.</td><td>A general service error while you check the key.</td></tr><tr><td>429</td><td>Throws an exception.</td><td>A limit message appropriate to the account error.</td></tr><tr><td>Network failure or 5xx</td><td>Throws an exception.</td><td>A temporary lookup failure with a retry option.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Catch exceptions at the application boundary and log the status needed to diagnose them. Keep authentication headers out of logs. The <a href="/docs/rate-limits/">limits guide</a> explains the difference between a temporary request limit and an exhausted lookup allowance.</p>
<h2 id="display">Display product fields safely in a PHP page</h2>
<p>When placing a returned name or brand into HTML, escape the text for that context. Product fields come from external data sources. For example, render a name with <code>htmlspecialchars($name, ENT_QUOTES | ENT_SUBSTITUTE, "UTF-8")</code>. The command-line example above prints plain text and does not render HTML.</p>
<p>Keep the key on your server and protect your lookup route with the access controls and usage limits appropriate to your application. Let a user confirm a matched product before changing inventory records. Optional metadata may be missing, and the barcode lookup response contains product identity information rather than your stock balance.</p>
<h2 id="batch">Look up a supplier list from PHP</h2>
<p>For a list of identifiers, the <code>POST /v1/products/batch</code> endpoint accepts up to 100 barcodes and returns a result for each item. Send a JSON object with a <code>barcodes</code> array of strings and a <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> header. Check your lookup allowance before importing a large list.</p>
<p>Read the <a href="/blog/batch-barcode-lookup-api/">batch barcode lookup guide</a> for a full request example. If your source is a spreadsheet, the <a href="/blog/enrich-barcode-csv-python/">Python CSV enrichment tutorial</a> provides a complete file-to-file workflow.</p>
<h2 id="next">Test a barcode from your application</h2>
<p><a href="/register/">Create a BarcodeNest account</a>, set your API key and run the PHP function with a barcode from your own catalog. Review the match and the fields your application needs. The <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a> lists the current plans for expanding usage.</p></div></div></BlogPostLayout>
